graph TB
    subgraph "Action Architecture"
        A[Action Client] --> B[Goal Request]
        B --> C[Action Server]
        C --> D[Goal Processing]
        D --> E[Feedback Loop]
        E --> F[Result Delivery]
        F --> A
        G[Cancellation Request] --> C
        C --> H[Goal Cancellation]
        H --> A
    end

    subgraph "Action Components"
        I[Goal Message] --> C
        J[Feedback Message] --> E
        K[Result Message] --> F
    end

    subgraph "State Management"
        L[Goal State: PENDING] --> M[Goal State: ACTIVE]
        M --> N[Goal State: SUCCEEDED]
        M --> O[Goal State: CANCELED]
        M --> P[Goal State: ABORTED]
        G --> O
    end

    subgraph "Lifecycle Node States"
        Q[Unconfigured] --> R[Inactive]
        R --> S[Active]
        S --> R
        R --> Q
        Q --> T[Finalized]
        S --> T
        R --> T
        U[Error State] -.-> Q
    end

    subgraph "Parameter Management"
        V[Parameter Declaration] --> W[Parameter Validation]
        W --> X[Parameter Updates]
        X --> Y[Dynamic Reconfiguration]
    end

    A -.-> V
    C -.-> V
    Y -.-> A
    Y -.-> C

    style A fill:#4CAF50,stroke:#388E3C,color:#fff
    style C fill:#2196F3,stroke:#0D47A1,color:#fff
    style D fill:#FF9800,stroke:#E65100,color:#fff
    style S fill:#9C27B0,stroke:#7B1FA2,color:#fff

%% Accessibility: This diagram illustrates the ROS 2 action architecture showing how action clients send goals to action servers, receive feedback during processing, and get final results. It also shows the state management for goals (PENDING, ACTIVE, SUCCEEDED, CANCELLED, ABORTED) and cancellation capabilities.